1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,096
Imagine you are an ant on an apple.

2
00:00:02,096 --> 00:00:04,133
You will never leave its surface.

3
00:00:04,133 --> 00:00:07,247
How could you ever learn what shape your world has?

4
00:00:07,247 --> 00:00:13,117
In 1904, the mathematician Henri Poincaré asked a question like this about our own kind of space.

5
00:00:13,117 --> 00:00:15,752
It took almost one hundred years to answer.

6
00:00:15,752 --> 00:00:22,400
The answer is one of the great stories in science, and you do not need to know any advanced math to follow it.

7
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:24,396
Here is a clever test.

8
00:00:24,396 --> 00:00:28,963
Walk anywhere, unrolling a rope behind you, and come back to the start.

9
00:00:28,963 --> 00:00:30,168
Now pull the rope.

10
00:00:30,168 --> 00:00:32,896
On an apple, it always slides back to you.

11
00:00:32,896 --> 00:00:37,019
On a doughnut, if you went around the hole, the rope gets stuck.

12
00:00:37,019 --> 00:00:41,903
So without ever leaving its world, an ant can tell an apple from a doughnut.

13
00:00:41,903 --> 00:00:48,120
For surfaces like these, if the rope always comes back, your world is a sphere, up to stretching.

14
00:00:48,720 --> 00:00:50,197
Now, the real question.

15
00:00:50,197 --> 00:00:53,471
Imagine a whole universe, finite but with no edge.

16
00:00:53,471 --> 00:00:57,901
You fly a rocket on any journey, trailing a rope, and you come back.

17
00:00:57,901 --> 00:01:04,449
If the rope can always be pulled in, must your universe be the three-dimensional version of a sphere?

18
00:01:04,449 --> 00:01:09,071
We cannot picture that shape, because it does not fit inside our space.

19
00:01:09,071 --> 00:01:11,704
But mathematics can describe it exactly.

20
00:01:11,704 --> 00:01:14,400
That question is the Poincaré conjecture.

21
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:16,670
Here is the strange part.

22
00:01:16,670 --> 00:01:19,141
Higher dimensions were solved first.

23
00:01:19,141 --> 00:01:22,814
In 1961, Stephen Smale settled dimensions five and up.

24
00:01:22,814 --> 00:01:26,153
In 1982, Michael Freedman settled dimension four.

25
00:01:26,153 --> 00:01:31,228
Dimension three, the one that looks like our own space, was still standing.

26
00:01:31,228 --> 00:01:38,040
There is too little room to move things around, and yet plenty of room for shapes to get complicated.

27
00:01:38,640 --> 00:01:41,722
Then came a beautiful idea from Richard Hamilton.

28
00:01:41,722 --> 00:01:44,742
Think of a metal plate with hot and cold spots.

29
00:01:44,742 --> 00:01:48,138
Heat spreads, and the differences fade by themselves.

30
00:01:48,138 --> 00:01:51,032
What if the shape of space could do the same?

31
00:01:51,032 --> 00:01:53,925
Hamilton wrote an equation called Ricci flow.

32
00:01:53,925 --> 00:01:58,580
Bumpy geometry tends to even out over time, a little like heat spreading.

33
00:01:58,580 --> 00:02:02,480
A lumpy world could smooth itself into a shape you recognize.

34
00:02:03,080 --> 00:02:04,491
But there was a catch.

35
00:02:04,491 --> 00:02:07,890
Picture a dumbbell: two balls joined by a thin neck.

36
00:02:07,890 --> 00:02:11,482
The neck shrinks faster than the balls, and it pinches.

37
00:02:11,482 --> 00:02:12,829
The equation breaks.

38
00:02:12,829 --> 00:02:14,689
Hamilton's plan was surgery.

39
00:02:14,689 --> 00:02:19,114
Just before the neck pinches, cut it, cap both ends, and keep going.

40
00:02:19,114 --> 00:02:22,898
The hard part was proving this could always be controlled.

41
00:02:22,898 --> 00:02:25,720
Nobody could do it, for about twenty years.

42
00:02:26,320 --> 00:02:30,886
Then, in 2002 and 2003, Grigori Perelman posted three papers online.

43
00:02:30,886 --> 00:02:34,244
He showed how to keep this process under control.

44
00:02:34,244 --> 00:02:39,281
Near a trouble spot, the shape looks like one of just a few simple models.

45
00:02:39,281 --> 00:02:46,063
Each cut removes a definite amount of volume, so the cuts cannot pile up endlessly in a finite time.

46
00:02:46,063 --> 00:02:48,682
Experts spent years checking his work.

47
00:02:48,682 --> 00:02:52,711
He was offered the Fields Medal and a million-dollar prize.

48
00:02:52,711 --> 00:02:53,920
He declined both.

49
00:02:54,520 --> 00:02:56,990
Here is the ending, and it is gorgeous.

50
00:02:56,990 --> 00:03:02,691
If your world passes the rope test, the flow makes everything disappear in a finite time.

51
00:03:02,691 --> 00:03:05,035
Every piece rounds off and vanishes.

52
00:03:05,035 --> 00:03:06,808
Now run the movie backward.

53
00:03:06,808 --> 00:03:11,496
Those vanishing pieces are simple shapes, and each cut can be glued back.

54
00:03:11,496 --> 00:03:15,803
When you rebuild the world, the only thing you can get is a sphere.

55
00:03:15,803 --> 00:03:19,160
So the rope test was enough, just as Poincaré asked.

56
00:03:19,760 --> 00:03:21,724
Today there is a new chapter.

57
00:03:21,724 --> 00:03:28,497
In 2026, a team reported a proof of this theorem in Lean, a program that checks logic step by step.

58
00:03:28,497 --> 00:03:32,290
MathDB marks one version of that work as Lean verified.

59
00:03:32,290 --> 00:03:35,000
Let's be careful about what this means.

60
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:37,844
We did not run that huge check ourselves.

61
00:03:37,844 --> 00:03:44,821
And a computer check does not explain the idea, or prove that the code asks Poincaré's exact question.

62
00:03:44,821 --> 00:03:46,650
People have to check that.

63
00:03:46,650 --> 00:03:51,120
The theorem itself already rests on two decades of expert review.

64
00:03:51,720 --> 00:03:53,339
So here is the whole story.

65
00:03:53,339 --> 00:03:55,677
An ant can test its world with a rope.

66
00:03:55,677 --> 00:04:00,053
Let the shape smooth itself, cut the necks, and watch everything vanish.

67
00:04:00,053 --> 00:04:02,451
Run it backward, and you find a sphere.

68
00:04:02,451 --> 00:04:06,767
A question about shape was answered by watching shape change over time.

69
00:04:06,767 --> 00:04:10,904
All the mathematics here belongs to the people named in the sources.

70
00:04:10,904 --> 00:04:13,722
This explanation was made with the help of AI.

71
00:04:13,722 --> 00:04:15,640
Sources are in the description.
